#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-267" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value(s) of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-1$?\n${\\frac{x^{3} + 1}{kx + k},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne - 1 \\\\ \\frac{k}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x =-1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{3} + 1}{kx + k} &= ((x + 1)(x^{2} - x + 1))/(k(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 1)(x^{2} - x + 1))/(k(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2} - x + 1}{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can compute the limit at $x=-1$, and get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} g(x) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} \\left(\\dfrac{x^2-x+1}{k}\\right) = \\dfrac{3}{k}\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 1)g(x) &= g(-1) \\\\\n\\frac{3}{k} &= \\frac{k}{3} \\\\\n9 &= k^{2} \\\\\n± 3 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-1$, the value of $k$ must be either $-3$ or $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" :question/math-academy-id "e-268" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the values of $k$ for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$.\n${\\frac{x^{2} + 4x - 12}{x^{2} - 4},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\\\ 2^{kx},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 2$." :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})\\frac{x^{2} + 4x - 12}{x^{2} - 4} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2^{-})((x - 2)(x + 6))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2^{-})((x - 2)(x + 6))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2^{-})\\frac{x + 6}{x + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 + 6}{2 + 2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})2^{kx} \\\\\n&= 2^{2k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n\n$$\nf(2) = 2^{2k}\n$$\n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) \\\\\n2^{2k} &= 2 \\\\\n2^{2k} &= 2^{1} \\\\\n2k &= 1 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nremoves the discontinuity at $x=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2d497f-046e-5ff8-9cc4-36ed95644117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099802} {:answer/id #uuid "932f68d0-3981-5043-bca8-0d34e2a7c58c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099803} {:answer/id #uuid "b489cad0-a823-5608-8178-c647f5a4a9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099804} {:answer/id #uuid "139fc8af-4064-5873-aff1-d72d9a72cf0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099805} {:answer/id #uuid "200d7f0a-daed-5888-8a11-0597758e9c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099806}] :answer-field/id #uuid "81d7abef-1e5f-5e7e-a713-da16f17f6fd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099801}] :question/id #uuid "ab43e107-00fc-5630-8abd-2dc86272117f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48743" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x =-2$?\n$g(x) = {\\frac{x^{2} + 5x + 6}{kx + 2k},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne - 2 \\\\ k,\\end{vmatrix}x =-2$"}]}
